~ A group obeys capability footprints like any other named state, and a cursor
~ charges the group it walks — so `update Health of Body` is a write to Bodies,
~ not to some loop variable the checker cannot see.
~
~ This matters more than the usual footprint case. A system that iterates every
~ entity in the world is exactly the code you most want to be able to audit by
~ reading its header, so "which systems can write Bodies?" has to stay a grep.
~
~ Here the action declares Console and nothing else, then reads a group. Rejected
~ before the program runs.
create group "Bodies" with
  "X" of type int,
  "Health" of type int >= 0

insert element 1, 10 to Bodies

create action "Peek" writes Console as
  loop over Bodies as Body
    print(X from Body)
  end
end

Peek()
